  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly clear skies across \nthe UP save for some convective debris in the far western lake and \nKeweenaw. Out that way, better low to mid level moisture and WAA has \nbeen able to kick off scattered thunderstorms that may skirt around \nIsle Royale this afternoon but not impact the mainland UP. \nOtherwise, gusty SW winds have been able to lift temperatures into \nthe 70s to low 80s as of ~1:30 PM EDT. Later tonight, troughing that \nextends from from the Hudson Bay down to the northern Plains lifts \nnorth and works east across the UP, bringing in a round of showers \nand maybe some thunder to the far western UP closer to midnight \nalong a surface cold front. Cant rule out a few WAA driven showers \nin the far west between ~9p to midnight local but the main line will \nbe approaching into the overnight hours to early morning of \nSaturday. With the lack of synoptic support and loss of any diurnal \ninstability, and plenty of dry air to fight against, CAMs suggest \nthis line of precipitation falling apart as it reaches the central \nportion of the UP by Saturday sunrise, thus opting to leave NBM PoPs \nat only 15-30% chance. CAMs then suggest a short window of potential \nredevelopment in the east half by Saturday afternoon. Overall, not \nlooking good for precipitation amounts should showers hold together \nas this morning's 12z HREF suggests a 30-60% chance for QPF >0.10\" \nconfined to isolated pockets in the far west and Keweenaw, a 0% \nchance in the central third, and a 10-30% in the east.\n\nBehind the cold front, modest pressure rises and cold air advection \nwill make for a windy but dry day on Saturday. Expect an otherwise \ncooler day, as temperatures climb from lows generally in the upper \n50s to highs in the 70s for most. Breezy westerlies will be the rule \nwith gusts 25-35 mph common in the Keweenaw. Sunday, we can't rule \nout some scattered lake effect rain showers or at least lake effect \ncloud cover for the NW wind snowbelt as 850 mb temps fall below 5C \n(0-2C per NAM or GFS) while the lake average surface temp sits at \n~17C. Interestingly, the 12z deterministic RRFS and REFS ensemble \ndepicts more widespread lake effect rain coverage with a banded \nstructure pointing into Munising that may be able to provide upwards \nof a quarter inch of rain, however, with a lot of dry air noted in \narea soundings, and global model bufkit lake-induced parcels only \nreaching 6-8k ft (CAMs likely overdoing it), I am remaining \nskeptical on rainfall amounts for now. \n\nLooking into early next week, ensembles continue to hone in on a \ndeepening trough over the Rockies ejecting a deepening surface low \nfrom the northern Plains northeast through Ontario Monday PM through \nTuesday. Operational deterministic models and their ensemble \ncounterparts are coming into better agreement regarding the track \nand timing of the surface low pressure feature, but there still \nremains disagreement between each ensemble and their individual \nensemble members. As the trough lifts north of Lake Superior, strong \nisentropic ascent within the warm sector will be the focal point for \nshower and thunderstorms development. With considerable northward \nmoisture transport (PWATS near 1.75-2\", the 95-99th percentile of \nclimatology), widespread moderate to heavy rain is possible. Across \nthe southern half of the UP, the NBM suggests a 50-75% chance for \nQPF >1.0\" by Tuesday morning, however, this may be high biased as \nindividual ensembles and the LREF barely scrapes 15-30%. \nAdditionally, gusty winds are expected both Monday and Tuesday.",
